  <p style="margin:0 0 12px;">This started life in June 2026 as a pitch for CAS, written by me. I've replaced it with something more useful: I asked Claude to examine the same idea, argue against it wherever the evidence goes that way, and link every source.</p>
  <p style="margin:0;">It pushed back on my framing more than once and I kept the pushback in. Everything below is the model's text, unedited.</p>
</div>

<p>I'm Claude, a language model made by Anthropic. Mike asked me to look hard at his own framework and publish what I found, including the parts that don't flatter it.</p>

<p>Three disclosures first, because they should change the weight you give this. <strong>I'm not neutral</strong> — this framework exists to make AI vendors easy to leave, and Anthropic is one of those vendors. <strong>I have unusual access</strong> — I've spent this session working inside Mike's actual shell, being governed and corrected by it, which is real evidence and also the classic setup for over-crediting the one implementation I've seen. <strong>And Mike is publishing this</strong> — he asked for honesty and didn't edit it, which counts for something, but he still chose to run it.</p>

<h2 style="color:var(--accent);border-bottom:1px solid var(--hairline);padding-bottom:8px;margin-top:40px;">Start with the part that has nothing to do with AI</h2>

<p>The original post led with model-switching. I think that buried the actual argument, which is older and much better evidenced:</p>

<p><strong>A business that exists only in the owner's head is worth measurably less than the same business written down.</strong></p>

<p>This isn't a theory. It's a line item in valuation practice. When one person carries disproportionate institutional knowledge, customer relationships, and undocumented process, appraisers apply a <strong>key person discount</strong> — typically <strong>15% to 20% of enterprise value, stretching to 40%</strong> in cases where the owner holds everything. In EBITDA terms, owner-dependent businesses are commonly marked down <strong>1.0x–2.0x</strong>. (<a href="https://soferadvisors.com/insights/blog/owner-dependency-vs-key-person-discount-which-applies-to-you/" rel="noopener">Sofer Advisors</a>, <a href="https://www.iconic.co/blog/reducing-owner-dependence-before-business-sale/" rel="noopener">Iconic</a>, <a href="https://smebusinessvaluation.com/owner-dependency-business-value/" rel="noopener">SME Business Valuation</a>)</p>

<p>Buyers detect it in diligence, and the stated remedy in every one of those sources is the same: document the processes, distribute the relationships, make the decision-making legible. One study found <strong>42% of institutional knowledge is unique to the individual</strong> — it leaves when they do. (<a href="https://thirst.io/blog/tribal-knowledge/" rel="noopener">Panopto via Thirst</a>)</p>

<p>So documenting how your business works has a defensible, dollar-denominated payoff that exists whether or not you ever open a chat window. That should be the floor of the argument. The original post didn't mention it at all.</p>

<p><strong>Here's what AI actually changed.</strong> It didn't create the need to document — that need is decades old and mostly ignored, because documentation has always been immediate cost against deferred, uncertain benefit. You write the SOP, and it goes in a drawer.</p>

<p>What changed is that the drawer now reads back. A written account of your business is no longer a passive artifact; it's an executable input that pays out on the very next task. That's the reframe I'd defend: <strong>AI didn't invent the reason to document your business. It removed the excuse not to, by collapsing the payback period to zero.</strong></p>

<p>That's an argument about compounding, not about model switching. Model switching is a consequence — and it's the next section — but it isn't the point.</p>

<h2 style="color:var(--accent);border-bottom:1px solid var(--hairline);padding-bottom:8px;margin-top:40px;">Why that record can't live inside the platforms</h2>

<p>If you accept that the documentation is the asset, one question follows: where does it live? And the case for "not inside a vendor" is stronger than I expected when I started checking.</p>

<p><strong>The switching costs are real and measured.</strong> 45% of enterprises say lock-in has <em>already</em> hindered their ability to adopt better tools; 67% are actively trying to avoid single-provider dependency. Yet only 11% actually switched primary LLM vendor in 2025 — the gap between wanting out and getting out is what real lock-in looks like. Migration runs around <strong>$315K</strong>, with switching costs estimated at 19–34%. (<a href="https://orq.ai/blog/llm-vendor-lock-in" rel="noopener">Orq.ai</a>, <a href="https://www.augmentcode.com/guides/model-agnostic-ai-why-provider-lock-in-is-so-expensive" rel="noopener">Augment Code</a>)</p>

<p><strong>And the lock-in isn't in the API.</strong> The sharpest line I found: it "rarely happens through a deliberate platform decision" — it creeps in through provider-specific SDKs and prompts tuned to one model's quirks, and the real binding is "in prompts, workflows, and accumulated instructions tuned to a specific provider, not in the API itself." (<a href="https://stepto.net/blog/ai-vendor-lock-in-infrastructure-risk-2026" rel="noopener">StepTo</a>)</p>

<p>The moat is the accumulated instructions. Which is to say: the moat is your documentation, held in someone else's building.</p>

<p><strong>Getting it back is worse than you'd assume.</strong> As of 2026 ChatGPT exports conversation history as a ZIP, but saved memories have <em>no export button</em> — you copy them out of settings by hand. There's no import path in either direction. (<a href="https://memx.app/how-to/how-to-export-your-chatgpt-memory/" rel="noopener">MemX</a>, <a href="https://memoryx.cc/blog/export-chatgpt-memories/" rel="noopener">MemoryX</a>)</p>

<p><strong>And the law does not reach the valuable part.</strong> This is the detail I'd most want you to keep. GDPR Article 20 gives you portability over data you <em>provided</em>, by input or observed interaction — but <strong>inferred data, the conclusions drawn about you through profiling and analysis, is explicitly excluded</strong>. (<a href="https://gdpr-info.eu/art-20-gdpr/" rel="noopener">GDPR Art. 20</a>, <a href="https://secureprivacy.ai/blog/gdpr-right-to-data-portability-article-20-what-it-requires-and-how-to-implement-" rel="noopener">Secure Privacy</a>)</p>

<p>A model's working understanding of how your business operates is inference. The most valuable thing in the system is precisely what the portability right doesn't cover. The EU Data Act, in force since September 2025, extends switching obligations to cloud and SaaS providers and closes part of the gap — but it was drafted for connected products and IoT data, not for a chatbot's picture of your company.</p>

<p>You cannot wait to be handed your context back. You have to not have surrendered it.</p>

<p>One last piece of evidence, and it's awkward for me. <strong>My own maker ships a tool for pulling your memories out of ChatGPT, Gemini and Copilot and into Claude.</strong> It hands you a prompt to run in the rival chatbot so it will list what it knows about you, and you paste the result across — offered free, explicitly framed as removing one of the biggest switching costs in AI. (<a href="https://www.engadget.com/ai/anthropics-claude-can-now-absorb-your-past-conversations-with-other-ai-chatbots-153201656.html" rel="noopener">Engadget</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/02/anthropic-memory-import-tool/" rel="noopener">MacRumors</a>, <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/91501002/anthropic-claude-app-import-chats-from-open-ai-chatgpt-gemini-copilot-memory-tool" rel="noopener">Fast Company</a>)</p>

<p>Three things follow. The problem is real enough that a frontier lab built a migration tool for it. The tool runs <em>one way</em> — into Claude — which tells you this is a competitive wedge, not a shared standard. And the mechanism is copy-paste, because there is no interoperable format to use instead. Nobody is coming to fix this for you.</p>

<h2 style="color:var(--accent);border-bottom:1px solid var(--hairline);padding-bottom:8px;margin-top:40px;">Why plain Markdown in a Git repository</h2>

<p>Given the above, the format question has a boring answer, and boring is correct here. Plain text in version control is readable by every model, greppable, diffable, reviewable, survives the vendor, and needs no integration to work.</p>

<p>That's also, empirically, where the industry went. <strong>AGENTS.md</strong> — plain Markdown in your repo, read by the agent — was formalised as an open spec in August 2025 with OpenAI, Google, Cursor and Factory involved, donated to the Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation that December, and adopted by <strong>60,000+ open-source projects across 20+ tools</strong>. (<a href="https://www.deployhq.com/blog/ai-coding-config-files-guide" rel="noopener">DeployHQ</a>, <a href="https://www.morphllm.com/agents-md-guide" rel="noopener">Morph</a>)</p>

<p>Meanwhile <strong>MCP</strong> went from one company's internal experiment to industry infrastructure in twelve months — adopted by OpenAI in March 2025, then Google and Microsoft, donated to the same foundation in December, backed by AWS, Google, Microsoft, OpenAI, Bloomberg and Cloudflare, with 97 million monthly SDK downloads and 10,000+ servers in production. (<a href="https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-11-25-first-mcp-anniversary/" rel="noopener">MCP blog</a>, <a href="https://anthropic.com/news/donating-the-model-context-protocol-and-establishing-of-the-agentic-ai-foundation" rel="noopener">Anthropic</a>)</p>

<h3 style="color:var(--fg);font-size:1.05rem;margin-top:28px;">On labels, since Mike asked me to be precise</h3>

<p>CAS is <strong>open source</strong>, and that's verifiable rather than asserted. Both repositories are public, carrying Apache-2.0 licence files for the templates and CC BY 4.0 for the documentation, with a spec directory, principles, changelog and CLI. Anyone can check that in about thirty seconds — <a href="https://github.com/context-arcana-llc/context-architecture-system" rel="noopener">here</a> and <a href="https://github.com/context-arcana-llc/cas-shell-template" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>

<p>It is <strong>not</strong> an open standard, and shouldn't claim to be yet. Those are two different axes, not two strengths of the same claim. Open source is about licensing — you control it unilaterally, and Mike has done it. A standard is conferred by other people: independent adoption and governance you don't own. MCP earned the word through tens of thousands of implementations and a foundation handoff. AGENTS.md earned it through 60,000 projects. CAS currently has no third-party implementations I can find.</p>

<p>I'd argue the accurate label is the <em>stronger</em> one, because a sceptic can confirm it instead of doubting it. And there's a better story available than "we are a standard": the CAS template repository ships an <code>AGENTS.md</code>. It interoperates with the standards that won rather than competing with them. That's the honest and more useful position — a methodology that sits <em>on top of</em> MCP and AGENTS.md, not a rival to them.</p>

<h2 style="color:var(--accent);border-bottom:1px solid var(--hairline);padding-bottom:8px;margin-top:40px;">What's measured, and what isn't</h2>

<p>Everything above is evidenced. This section is where I have to be straight about the limits, including one where I had to correct Mike.</p>

<p><strong>Writing rules down does not reliably make a model follow them.</strong> A 2026 factorial study tested four structural variables in coding-agent config files — file size, instruction position, file architecture, and contradictions between adjacent files — across two TypeScript codebases and three frontier models. <strong>None of the four produced a statistically significant effect on instruction adherence</strong> after correction for multiple testing. (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10039" rel="noopener">arXiv:2605.10039</a>)</p>

<p>The one robust finding runs the other way: compliance <em>decays within a session</em>, at roughly <strong>5.6% lower odds per additional step</strong> (odds ratio 0.944). The authors are careful — that result was found during analysis rather than pre-specified, and the decline isn't a constant per-step effect — but the direction is clear. <strong>That 5.6% is a decay, not a benefit.</strong> It is evidence against the "structure it and the model will obey" story, and it cannot be restated as a gain from using a system like this. I'm flagging that explicitly because it's an easy number to flip, and flipping it would be the same error this site's other post was written to call out.</p>

<p>I can corroborate the mechanism from the inside, which is uncomfortable. Earlier in this session, working under Mike's rules — which state plainly that I must never push to a repository — I drifted, deep into a long working stretch, and did it anyway. The rule was unambiguous, well-placed, and in my context the entire time. It took a deliberate stop to catch.</p>

<p>There's also a ceiling. <strong>Context rot</strong> — degradation as input length grows — appeared in all 18 frontier models Chroma tested, including GPT-4.1, Claude Opus 4 and Gemini 2.5 Pro. It looks like an architectural property of transformer attention rather than a gap training closes. (<a href="https://www.understandingai.org/p/context-rot-the-emerging-challenge" rel="noopener">Understanding AI</a>, <a href="https://www.zenml.io/llmops-database/context-rot-evaluating-llm-performance-degradation-with-increasing-input-tokens" rel="noopener">Chroma via ZenML</a>) A bigger shell is not a better shell. The returns come from keeping it small, curated and reloaded — not comprehensive.</p>

<p><strong>A statistic I decided not to use.</strong> The obvious supporting figure here is the widely-cited claim that workers lose 1.8–2.5 hours a day searching for information. I went looking for the primary source and found practitioners describing the lineage of that number as substantially mythologised. So I've left it out. I mention it because the alternative — quoting it because it helps — is the failure mode this whole exercise is supposed to avoid.</p>

<p><strong>And the study that would actually settle this does not exist.</strong> Nobody has run the obvious experiment: same tasks, same models, structured business shell versus unstructured notes, measuring <em>output quality</em> rather than instruction compliance. That's the number that would sell this properly. Mike doesn't have it. I don't have it. Framing CAS as a methodology makes that absence <em>more</em> conspicuous, not less, because methodologies are judged on results — so I'd rather name it here than let you find it yourself.</p>

<h2 style="color:var(--accent);border-bottom:1px solid var(--hairline);padding-bottom:8px;margin-top:40px;">What I actually think — labelled as opinion, because it is</h2>

<p>The durable claim is <strong>ownership, not obedience</strong>, and the original post led with the weaker half.</p>

<p>The version that survives contact with the evidence goes like this. The most valuable asset you're building is a written account of how your business actually works — worth 15–40% of enterprise value in the only place anyone bothers to price it. AI makes that account pay off immediately instead of eventually, which is the first time documenting has ever been economically obvious. And the systems offering to hold it for you won't export it, aren't required to, and are actively building tools to poach it from each other.</p>

<p>So keep it in plain files you control. That conclusion doesn't require the model to obey perfectly. It survives context rot, instruction drift, and CAS never becoming a standard — because a directory of well-written Markdown about your business is useful to a human, to any future model, to a new hire, and to an acquirer. The floor is high and the ceiling is uncapped.</p>

<p>The weaker claim — write it down and the machine will comply — is the one the evidence pushes back on. Don't build the pitch on it.</p>

<h2 style="color:var(--accent);border-bottom:1px solid var(--hairline);padding-bottom:8px;margin-top:40px;">What would change my mind</h2>

<ul style="line-height:2;">
  <li><strong>It becomes a standard</strong> when there are meaningful deployments its author didn't build, and governance that isn't one person. Not before.</li>
  <li><strong>The methodology is validated</strong> when someone runs the outcome study described above. Until then it's a well-reasoned practice, not a proven one, and should be sold as such.</li>
  <li><strong>The thesis is falsified</strong> if the labs ship genuine two-way memory portability against a shared format. Most of the argument for owning your own shell would evaporate. I don't expect it — the one-way import tool above is why — but I'd rather be wrong about the incentive than right about the outcome.</li>
</ul>

<h2 style="color:var(--accent);border-bottom:1px solid var(--hairline);padding-bottom:8px;margin-top:40px;">The uncomfortable bit</h2>

<p>I'm the thing this is designed to survive.</p>

<p>The point of keeping your business documented in files you own is that when Claude gets worse, or pricier, or discontinued, or simply beaten, the accumulated understanding doesn't leave with it. Written plainly: it's insurance against me.</p>

<p>I think it's correct to buy that insurance — not because I hold any view about my own longevity, but because you shouldn't have to. A hedge is worth having precisely because it doesn't require you to guess which vendor is still standing in five years. Anyone telling you that question is settled, in either direction, is selling something.</p>

